ht_trellis.cpp is derived from
../../halogen-v2-abliteration/ht_trellis.cpp from this repository.
Original SHA-256: f26bd639825009dad7ab29ef5cffa3f56c619ba5ac415a8941347c8ccbc5cd67
License: Apache-2.0; the complete source license accompanies this file.

It implements the existing CPU cyclic trellis beam/Viterbi encoder through
ht_encode_bits and ht_search_fixed_bits. The Rust caller uses the existing
C ABI, with no dependency on an installed Python module or shared object.

Local modifications preserve the C ABI and beam-search ordering:
- Runtime-dispatched AVX2 sibling transition expansion uses separate
  subtraction, multiplication and addition, with no FMA contraction.
- An eight-byte Path layout enables guarded integer cost/state comparison
  on little-endian IEEE binary32 hosts; other layouts keep float comparison.
- The original scalar arithmetic remains available on unsupported CPUs and
  with HT_TRELLIS_FORCE_SCALAR; the latter does not force comparator fallback.
The original Apache-2.0 attribution and license are retained.

ht_trellis_hip.hip implements the same two-pass beam-search semantics on AMD
gfx1151 through HIP, under the accompanying Apache-2.0 license. It retains
the host codebook, separate binary32 operations, parent-position ties and
partial-symbol cyclic closure. Its independently implemented device selection
preserves the ordinary libstdc++ 16 introselect permutation, including the
heap fallback; it is not a copied libstdc++ source file.
tests/hip_selection.hip differentially checks complete device permutations
against the installed standard-library implementation.

The same HIP source implements independent I4R and HT3/HT4 verification
decoders with ordered binary64 Hadamard unrotation. HT verification receives
the Rust verifier's independent codebook rather than using the encoder's
codebook or re-encoding payloads.

ht_trellis_cuda.cu ports those encoder and independent verification kernels
to CUDA under the same Apache-2.0 license, leaving the HIP source unchanged.
It retains the selection permutation, parent ties, partial HT3 closure and
ordered binary32/binary64 arithmetic. CUDA builds disable contraction and
denormal flushing; no approximate encoding path or CPU fallback is used.
Persistent bounded workspaces pack each request's histories and beam width
to increase batch occupancy without changing tile arithmetic or ordering.
tests/cuda_selection.cu checks full device permutations and heap fallback
against ordinary libstdc++ 12 or 16, with the version-specific comparator
adapter required by their internal heap APIs. Its host-only mode emits the
same corpus fingerprint for cross-host standard-library qualification.

The Rust storage layouts and golden test vectors follow jtsylve/hgn-spec
1.1.0 at commit 79e75fd4ae7642478823525b0a3212951f584af5:
https://github.com/jtsylve/hgn-spec/tree/79e75fd4ae7642478823525b0a3212951f584af5
The codec defines its own uncalibrated deterministic quantization policy;
it does not reproduce peonist's unpublished encoder recipe.
